/* the cards as first drawn (the Variants' "As first drawn": movement.js's setRev): each part whose pieces differ from the sheets as last revised takes these fields in place of
   its own (app.js's setRev keeps the others to put back). The pieces only the earlier sheets have are in PIECES above, each marked (as first drawn) */
const SH1=' As first drawn: the sheets of 1940-41, which the later revisions replaced (Wempe 3955 is made so).';
const REV={
  balance:{sn:'Its rim, arm and masses from their sheets; the 1941 staff and its brass collar from 05 04_5; its seat from 05 04_1; the masses and timing weights scaled on 05 03_2 and Wempe 3955',figs:'05 03_2, 05 03_4, 05 04_5, 05 04_1',
    d:'The balance: a cut bimetallic rim, nickel steel inside and brass outside, made in one with its straight steel arm (stamped RGG in a triangle, as on Wempe No. 3955), so each quarter of the rim curls in as it warms and carries its mass nearer the staff. Four round brass masses sit across the rim, two timing weights on screws at the arm’s ends. Its arm is riveted on the neck of the brass collar on its staff, whose flange is undercut 10°; the seat stands 4.1 over the top plate’s inner face, 0.3 higher than the 1946 staff puts it, and the hairspring rides higher with it. It swings about 1¼ turns, twice a second.'+SH1,
    sp:'Drawings 05 03 01, rim 05 03 01-1 (nickel steel and brass), staff 040401-1 with its collar 040401-2 (05 04_5, 4.4.41), layout 05 04_1 · rim Ø 32.32 × 3.8 · arm 1.15 thick · the seat 4.1 over the plate’s inner face'},
  rollers:{sn:'The impulse roller from its sheet; the discharge roller scaled on the layout; their heights from 05 04_1 and the 1941 collar',figs:'05 04_1, 05 04_6, 05 04_5',
    d:'Two rollers on the balance staff under the top plate, as 05 04_1 draws them: the impulse roller just under the 1941 collar’s tube, 0.6 under the plate and level with the escape wheel’s teeth, which lie just under the plate, and the discharge roller under it. The impulse roller carries the sapphire impulse stone, which a tooth of the escape wheel pushes on every other swing; the discharge stone pushes the gold spring and lifts the detent on that swing, and slips past it on the other.'+SH1,
    sp:'Drawings 02 04 02 (Heberolle, beryllium copper, Ø 6.78 × 1.6), 05 04 04-1 (not in the set) · stones 1.7 wide · 0.6 and 2.2 under the plate (05 04_1’s “1,6”, “1,6” under the collar’s 4.7)'},
  escapeWheel:{sn:'Its teeth, rim and crossings from its tooth-form sheet and the layout; its height from 05 04_1; its 1941 arbor and collet from 05 04_7.1',figs:'05 04_1, 05 04_7.1, 05 04_23',
    d:'The 15-tooth escape wheel just under the top plate, 0.5 from it, as 05 04_1 draws it and Wempe 3955 has it, on the 1941 arbor (05 04_7.1): 1.4 nearer the plate than the later arbor puts it. Held by the locking stone between impulses; released, it gives the balance’s impulse stone a push through about 20 degrees and comes to rest on the next tooth: a tooth every half second, so its pinion turns the seconds wheel once a minute. 05 04_7.1 draws its recess on the pinion’s side and Ø 12.4, which neither the layout nor the 1942 tooth form allows: drawn as theirs.'+SH1,
    sp:'Drawings 05 04_1 (layout, “0,5”, “1,4”), 050408 bis 10 (05 04_7.1: arbor, pinion z 10, collet 05 04 10), 02 04 08a (tooth form) · tips Ø 14.00 · bore 1.8 · arbor 23 −0.2'},
  detent:{sn:'Its lengths and place from 05 04_1; its sizes from Grießbach’s 1436, the first (unbracketed) figures; the gold spring from its sheet',figs:'05 04_1, 05 04_10.1, 05 04_2',
    d:'The detent as first drawn: a steel blade on a thin spring, its foot clamped under the edge of the block of 1941, 1.9 mm nearer the plate than as revised, to the first of 1436’s two sets of figures (the later, bracketed, sizes are thinner). The locking stone in its pipe holds a tooth of the escape wheel. On one swing the discharge stone pushes the gold spring against the detent’s horn and lifts the detent, freeing the wheel; on the other it only bends the gold spring aside.'+SH1,
    sp:'Layout 05 04_1 (detent 2 wide, 1.3 to 3.3 under the plate) · 1436’s first figures: foot 0.65 × 2 × 6.6, spring 0.07, square and blade 0.55, then 0.33, horn 0.25 × 0.8, pipe Ø 1.15 · gold spring 05 04 01-2 · stone Ø 0.75'},
  detentBlock:{src:'scaled',sn:'Its base and holes from 05 04 01-4, whose holes 05 04_1’s plan matches; the arm, its cove and the banking screw scaled on 05 04_1 (10:1) and measured on Wempe 3955’s photograph',figs:'05 04_1, 05 04_3',
    d:'The brass block of 1941 under the top plate that carries the detent, as on Wempe 3955: the same base as the later block, screwed up from the plate’s outer face through hole 13 and placed by two steady pins, the detent’s foot clamped to its edge; in place of the later upright a long arm, 2.8 thick on the base’s free side, reaching past the locking stone, its end slit to clamp the banking screw whose brass cone stands out past it.'+SH1,
    sp:'05 04_1 (no sheet of its own) · base 14.6 × 7.5 × 6.5 · arm 2.8 × 2.46, 3.7 to 6.5 under the plate, its end 2.9 past the stone · cone Ø 0.85 × 2.5'},
  escJewelUp:{d:'The escape wheel arbor’s upper hole jewel. The 1941 arbor’s pivot reaches 2.0 into the plate, so the setting fills the seat down to it, held by two screws whose heads lie over its edge.'+SH1},
  escJewelLow:{d:'The escape wheel arbor’s lower hole jewel, in a bush pressed into the pillar plate: shorter for the 1941 arbor, whose pivot ends 1.96 into the plate.'+SH1},
  escEndstoneLow:{d:'The endstone under the escape wheel arbor’s lower pivot: for the 1941 arbor, which ends inside the plate, in a plug set into the plate’s hole under the setting, which is then plain.'+SH1},
  barrel:{sn:'Every size from the 1941 sheets (0207, 020701, 020701-3); its place between the plate and the bridge from 0207’s section; the hook block and the slots the later drum’s',figs:'05 07_1, 05 07_2, 05 07_10',
    d:'The drum the mainspring is coiled in, as first drawn: three pieces, a brass ring with a lid sprung into its seat on the pillar plate’s side and a bottom on the barrel bridge’s, each with a boss standing inward round the arbor. Its outer end is hooked to the ring’s wall, its inner end to the arbor, which the set-up ratchet holds. Running, the spring turns the drum, which winds the chain onto itself off the fusee, about five turns over a wind.'+SH1,
    sp:'Drawings 0207 (Federhaus, 127-556.04 U24, 15.4.41), 020701 (U22), ring 020701-3 · Ø 41.5 × 18, inside Ø 38.5, 15.8 between lid and bottom · lid 1.2, bottom 1.0, seats Ø 39.2'},
  key:{src:'scaled',sn:'Traced on its 2:1 sheet (its 84 and 32 figured); its materials not given (estimated)',figs:'05 01_6',
    d:'The key of 1940 (Aufzugschlüssel), which the one-way key of 1943 replaced under the same number: a flat pear-shaped handle on a conical body and collar, the stem with its square socket pressed into it. It stands in the holder in the box’s back right corner.'+SH1,
    sp:'Drawing 127-556.02 U 01 (51 00 11, 12.12.40) · 84 overall, the stem Ø 6, 32 out · handle Ø 34.8 × 3.2'},
  /* figures the revisions changed by less than the model can show, or only in their tolerance: the shape is the same in both states, the card gives the first */
  ring:{sp:PARTS.ring.sp+' · As first drawn (before revision a, 6.12.46): 10.7 −0.05, the seat 3 ±0.05, the step 7 ±0.07 and 0.7 +0.05: the same sizes, closer tolerances'},
  lowerCock:{sp:PARTS.lowerCock.sp+' · As first drawn (before revision a, 2.12.46): the setting’s seat +0.05'},
  setupRatchet:{sp:PARTS.setupRatchet.sp+' · As first drawn (before revision b, 24.11.47): 2.63 −0.03 thick'},
  secondsWheel:{sp:PARTS.secondsWheel.sp+' · As first drawn (05 05 01-2 before 24.3.48): 24 −0.05 between its shoulders, the wheel’s seat 1.3 −0.1, the arbor Ø 1.55 untoleranced'},
  intermediateWheel:{sp:PARTS.intermediateWheel.sp+' · As first drawn (05 05 02, 1940): pivots Ø 0.82 −0.02'},
  changeWheel:{sp:PARTS.changeWheel.sp+' · As first drawn (040202-1, 1940): tip Ø 21.30 ±0.02 as printed (corrected in pencil to 21.05)'},
  hourWheel:{sp:PARTS.hourWheel.sp+' · As first drawn (040204-1, 1940): tip Ø 20.0 ±0.02 as printed (pencil 19.7), finished 1.0 thick (pencil 1.05 −0.03), turned Ø 20.1'},
  fusee:{sp:PARTS.fusee.sp+' · As first drawn, the fusee of 1941 (02 06 13, “Übergangsserie”, and its body 020613-2): the same groove, the flange Ø 36, its large end dished with a collar and a flange plate on a set screw in it, the bore tapered 1:70: not built (README)'},
  barrelArbor:{sn:'From its 1941 sheet, its core the sheet’s 12 between the 1941 drum’s bosses, its pivots and journals drawn to the plates and drum it runs in',figs:'05 07_9, 05 07_1',
    sp:'Drawing 020704-1 (127-556.04-093) · Präz. Rundstahl 14, hardened · core Ø 13.3 × 12, its ends 22½° · square 2.7'+' · as first drawn'}};
/* the pieces' cards as first drawn, by part and piece key: the earlier issue of the same line (05 04_5 carries 05 04 02-1 in pencil; 05 04_7.1 was renumbered 05 04 05) */
/* the cards as the Soviet 6MX has them (the Variants' Soviet 6MX, Kirov No. 247 of 1952; the Wempe README's "The Soviet 6MX"), as REV and REVP for the first drawn */
const K247='Kirov No. 247’s photographs (Altmeppen), fitted on the sheets’ hole table';
const REV6={
  topPlate:{sn:'The drawings’ plate; pillar 39’s place, the missing hole 53 and the chatons read off '+K247,src:'scaled',
    d:'The Soviet 6MX’s top plate, as Kirov No. 247’s: the drawings’ crescent with the third pillar moved from 210° to 190°, no hole for the balance holding work’s spring pin (53), and the intermediate and seconds arbors’ upper pivots in jewels in chatons, where the drawings press brass bushes. It carries the train’s upper pivots, the fusee’s bush, the two balance cocks and the barrel bridge, as the drawings’ does.',
    sp:'As 05 09 01 · Ø 85 × 3.35 · pillar holes on r 37 at 95° and 350°, the third at 190° on r 37.3 · chatons in the holes Ø 4.59 at 35 and 38'},
  pillarPlate:{sn:'The drawings’ plate; pillar 39’s place read off '+K247,src:'scaled',
    d:'The Soviet 6MX’s pillar plate: the drawings’ plate with the third pillar’s hole and seat moved from 210° to 190°, as Kirov No. 247’s dial side shows its screw. It carries the train’s lower pivots, the intermediate bridge and the motion work as the drawings’ does.',
    sp:'As 05 08 02-1 · Ø 85 × 3.35 · pillar holes Ø 4.50 on r 37 at 95° and 350°, the third at 190° on r 37.3'},
  pillars:{sn:'Every size from its sheet; the third pillar’s place read off '+K247+': 190° on both faces (±0.5°), r 37.3 to clear the seconds wheel’s tips by 0.2'},
  upperCock:{sn:'Every size from its sheet but the auxiliary hole 20, which the 6MX’s cock hasn’t ('+K247+')',
    d:'The cranked cock over the balance, screwed and pinned to the top plate, holding the balance staff’s upper jewel setting over the opening in the plate. The 6MX’s has no auxiliary hole (the “window” Altmeppen notes in the Wempe’s).'},
  intermediateBridge:{d:'A straight bridge on the pillar plate’s dial side, across the opening for the intermediate wheel: in the 6MX it carries that wheel’s lower jewel in a chaton and the seconds wheel’s lower jewel in its setting.'}};
/* the parts the 6MX shares with the first drawn (its escapement at the heights the sheets first drew, and the barrel with its cap underneath): their first-drawn cards, each
   saying why; the block and detent its own */
const FIRST6='The Soviet 6MX has this at the heights the sheets first drew (Kirov No. 247’s and Kirov-Poljot 24020’s photographs: the escape wheel just under the top plate, on a long cone collet), so as first drawn: ';
for(const k of['balance','rollers','escapeWheel','escJewelUp','escJewelLow','escEndstoneLow'])REV6[k]=Object.assign({},REV[k],{sn:FIRST6+REV[k].sn});
REV6.escapeWheel.sn+='; its collet one straight cone, Ø 2.77 to 1.70 over 5.1 (Kirov 247’s and Kirov-Poljot 24020’s detent views), where the sheets of 1941 draw a cone and a pipe';
for(const k of['barrel','barrelArbor'])REV6[k]=Object.assign({},REV[k],{sn:'The Soviet 6MX’s barrel cap is underneath (Altmeppen’s list), as the drawings’ barrel of 1941 has it, so as first drawn: '+REV[k].sn});
Object.assign(REV6,{
  detentBlock:{src:'scaled',sn:'05 04 01-4’s form, read in first angle as the sheet itself reads, which the 6MX’s block bears out; its stud and pins measured on Kirov No. 247’s and Kirov 009’s top plates (±0.4), its slide, stop screw, plain upright and the taper at its root on a loose 6MX block, the banking screw’s head on Kirov-Poljot 24020’s and 247’s',
    d:'The Soviet 6MX’s detent block: the revised block’s form, an L of brass under the top plate, its upright at the free face, so the detent hangs between it and the plate at the escape wheel’s height. It is held by a slotted steel stud flush in the plate’s outer face, 2.2 mm from where the drawings put the block’s screw, between two steady pins, and the stud passes through a gilt adjusting slide in a notch at the block’s corner. A large slotted screw in the block’s side beside the detent’s foot stands over its end, a stop for the foot. A screw through the upright, which has no slit, is the banking: its domed brass head on the wheel’s side of the detent, the pipe resting on it, sets the depth of lock. How the slide and stud adjust the block isn’t seen (the README’s Unresolved).',
    sp:'05 04 01-4 · base 14.6 × 7.5, 6.5 deep at its end and 3 beyond · upright 3 × 3, 3.5-6.5 under the plate · stud Ø 3.4 · stop screw head Ø 4.2 · banking screw M1.2, head Ø 3.2 × 1.4'},
  detent:{sn:'1436’s revised (bracketed) sizes, at the first-drawn heights the 6MX’s block holds it at; the 6MX’s own detent, shorter, not measured'}});
/* the 6MX's other parts that differ from the drawings' (X4): the dial's screws and the ring's holes for them, the striped bridges, the escapement's upper endstone at the first-drawn heights,
   the pillar plate without the hand-setting cut */
REV6.escEndstoneUp={sn:FIRST6+PARTS.escEndstoneUp.sn};
Object.assign(REV6.pillarPlate,{d:REV6.pillarPlate.d+' It has no hand-setting cut: the drawings’ window and holes 14-18 for it (which Wempe 3955’s plate has) aren’t on Kirov No. 247’s dial side, and Altmeppen lists no external hand-setting.'});
Object.assign(REV6,{
  dial:{sp:'As 05 02 00-4 · Ø 103.55 × 1.3 · screws at 354°, 114° and 234° (Kirov 247’s 353.1, 112.5, 233.6) on Ø 101'},   /* the plate's screws; its printing's text is the dial switch's (DIALC) */
  ring:{sn:'The drawings’ ring; its tapped holes for the dial’s screws moved with them (Kirov No. 247’s dial); its own screws’ places measured on both of Kirov 247’s faces (±0.5°), their heads on its side views',src:'scaled',
    d:'The movement ring as drawn, its three tapped holes for the dial’s screws at the 6MX dial’s places (114°, 234° and 354°). Its screws, which hold the pillar plate in its seat, are steel with low slotted heads and stand at 342°, 102° and 246°, not the drawings’ 340°, 100° and 220°. In the 6MX’s bowl it stands inside the threaded rim, the bezel’s shoulder on its flange.',
    sp:PARTS.ring.sp+' · the dial’s screws’ holes M1 at 114°, 234°, 354° on Ø 101 · its screws M2.6 at 342.2°, 102.2°, 246.4°, heads Ø 6.2 × 2.0'},
  lowerCock:{sn:'The drawings’ cock, cut back as every 6MX side view of the detent shows it (Kirov No. 247’s scaled by the drawn depth: 3.3 deep, 3.0-3.9); where the step stands estimated',src:'scaled',
    d:'The lower balance cock as drawn, but its bar is cut back to a third of its depth from the lug’s edge to its end, where the screw and the far pin are; only the part under the lug keeps the full 10 mm. The 6MX’s carry a number stamped on that deep part.',
    sp:PARTS.lowerCock.sp+' · cut back to 3.3 deep beyond the lug (6MX)'},
  barrelBridge:{sn:'The drawings’ bridge; its finish read off '+K247,src:'drawing',
    d:'The barrel bridge as drawn, finished as Kirov No. 247’s plates and bridges are: Geneva stripes 3.95 mm apart at 50°, running on across the top plate and cocks as one sweep (the Wempe’s are hammered).',
    sp:PARTS.barrelBridge.sp+' · Geneva stripes 3.95 apart (Kirov 247’s)'}});
